      "rev_n_2_c1565a_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_c1565a_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_c1565a",
        "thought_text": "The issue with the current approach is that it does not correctly implement the logic of the Thanos sort algorithm. Specifically, the dynamic programming table `dp` is being filled incorrectly. Instead of checking if the entire subarray is sorted, we need to consider the effect of snapping the fingers to remove either the first or the second half of the array. We should update `dp[start][end]` based on the maximum value between removing the first half (`dp[start + length // 2][end]`) and the second half (`dp[start][end - length // 2]`). Additionally, we need to ensure that the outer loop iterates over all possible lengths of subarrays and the inner loops correctly handle the starting points.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_c1565a_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_6d6be3c9cf",
        "created_at": 1794502828.9641566
      },
      "rev_n_3_f51c37_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_f51c37_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_f51c37",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the process of removing halves until a sorted array is obtained, keeping track of the maximum length. However, ensure that the algorithm correctly identifies the largest sorted subarray that can be obtained by snapping the fingers once. This involves checking both halves and selecting the longer sorted subarray each time.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_f51c37_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_07415155df",
        "created_at": 1794502847.332622
      },
